<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <BaseHead title="Agent Workflow Atlas - SuperAda" description="A public-safe atlas of 100 receipt-backed Enterprise Crew workflows across nine agents and twelve operating categories." />
  </head>
  <body>
    <Header />
    <main class="workflow-atlas">
      <section class="atlas-hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">SuperAda Field Guide</p>
        <h1>What an AI crew can do when every action leaves a receipt.</h1>
        <p class="lede">Browse the public-safe version of our v1 operating atlas: 100 workflow loops across research, communication, software, fleet work, content, business intelligence, media, governance, and personal support. The private proof ledger stays private; the reusable pattern and approval boundary are published.</p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a href="#catalog">Browse the catalog</a>
          <a href="#recipes" class="secondary">Copy a recipe</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="stat-grid" aria-label="Atlas summary">
        {stats.map((stat) => <article><span>{stat.label}</span><strong>{stat.value}</strong></article>)}
      </section>

      <section class="notice">
        <strong>Public edition.</strong> Internal proof links, private task URLs, customer/contact names, and raw source excerpts are not exposed here. Verified means the private ledger has a receipt. Inferred means the row is useful but still needs a direct public-safe receipt before it should be treated as proven.
      </section>

      <section class="visuals" aria-labelledby="visuals-title">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">Rich media map</p>
          <h2 id="visuals-title">Five visual primitives for the operating system</h2>
          <p>The diagrams show how the catalog works without leaking the underlying private receipts.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="visual-grid">
          {atlas.visuals.map((visual) => (
            <figure>
              <img src={visual.src} alt={visual.title} loading="lazy" />
              <figcaption>{visual.title}</figcaption>
            </figure>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="categories" class="categories" aria-labelledby="categories-title">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">Twelve lanes</p>
          <h2 id="categories-title">Start with the operating lane</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="category-grid">
          {atlas.categories.map((category) => (
            <article>
              <span>{category.id}</span>
              <h3>{category.name}</h3>
              <p>{category.promise}</p>
              <small>{category.visual_primitive}</small>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="recipes" class="recipes" aria-labelledby="recipes-title">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">Copyable loops</p>
          <h2 id="recipes-title">Recipes, not magic prompts</h2>
          <p>Replace the inputs. Keep the proof step. Do not remove the gate. Stunningly annoying how often that last bit saves everyone. 💀</p>
        </div>
        <div class="recipe-grid">
          <article><h3>Call transcript → tracked commitments</h3><p>Extract only explicit commitments. Return owner, due date, source quote or location, duplicate check, and whether it needs human approval. Do not send messages.</p><small>Gate: external follow-up requires approval.</small></article>
          <article><h3>Question → cited evidence dossier</h3><p>Answer from sources. Keep observations, inferences, and recommendations separate. Cite every material claim and name missing proof.</p><small>Gate: research and local drafting are autonomous.</small></article>
          <article><h3>Evidence bundle → public draft</h3><p>Turn evidence into a channel-ready draft. Preserve citations. Return a claim sheet and stop before publishing.</p><small>Gate: public delivery requires approval.</small></article>
          <article><h3>Validated task → bounded coding candidate</h3><p>Implement in an isolated worktree. Stay in scope. Run exact gates and return receipts, not vibes wearing a lab coat.</p><small>Gate: merge, push, deploy, and release are separate.</small></article>
          <article><h3>Health signal → reversible fleet repair</h3><p>Diagnose the live route first. Snapshot before change. Apply the smallest safe repair, then smoke-test and record before/after evidence.</p><small>Gate: destructive or critical-access actions need approval.</small></article>
          <article><h3>Daily metrics → decision pack</h3><p>Show period, denominator, revenue, cost, profit, risk, and recommended action in one view.</p><small>Gate: budget or commercial changes need approval.</small></article>
        </div>
      </section>


      <section id="usecases" class="usecases" aria-labelledby="usecases-title">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">Sixteen more in practice</p>
          <h2 id="usecases-title">Featured use cases, straight from the operator's week</h2>
          <p>The same recurring jobs, told plainly: what runs, what it connects to, what comes back, and where the human approval line sits.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="usecase-grid">
            <article class="usecase-card">
              <div class="uc-figure"><img src="/use-cases/ai-news-watch.webp" alt="Illustration: AI news watch" width="1024" height="576" loading="lazy" /></div>
              <div class="uc-topline"><span>Research</span><h3>AI news watch</h3><p>Spock and the WeeklyClaw research jobs scan AI news, remove duplicates, and turn useful developments into briefs or draft posts.</p></div>
              <dl>
                <div><dt>When it runs</dt><dd>Daily and twice-daily, depending on the lane. Some runs stay silent when there is nothing worth sending.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What it connects to</dt><dd>Hacker News, TechCrunch AI, The Verge AI, Ars Technica, credible X sources, Polymarket AI-market state, saved research memory, and prior draft history.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What Henry gets back</dt><dd>Ranked AI items with source links, why they matter, draft social posts when useful, and a clear marker when the evidence is weak or already covered.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Where the human stays in charge</dt><dd>Research and draft staging can run alone. Public posts, market-facing claims, customer references, or anything with shaky sourcing wait for review.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </article>
            <article class="usecase-card">
              <div class="uc-figure"><img src="/use-cases/public-safe-page.webp" alt="Illustration: Public-safe resource page" width="1024" height="576" loading="lazy" /></div>
              <div class="uc-topline"><span>Publishing</span><h3>Public-safe resource page</h3><p>Ada turns a private evidence pack into a page strangers can read without exposing private proof or internal mess.</p></div>
              <dl>
                <div><dt>When it runs</dt><dd>When a resource, use-case page, article, or public artifact needs to ship.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What it connects to</dt><dd>Private receipts, source notes, repo content, screenshots, diagrams, QA scripts, build output, deployment status, and production route checks.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What Henry gets back</dt><dd>A readable page, a privacy-safe version of the useful pattern, build receipts, route checks, and a clear list of anything blocked by approval or missing credentials.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Where the human stays in charge</dt><dd>Drafting, local build, and QA can run. Public release needs privacy review and approval for sensitive claims, private evidence, customer references, or external announcements.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </article>
            <article class="usecase-card">
              <div class="uc-figure"><img src="/use-cases/conference-followup.webp" alt="Illustration: Conference follow-up" width="1024" height="576" loading="lazy" /></div>
              <div class="uc-topline"><span>Sales</span><h3>Conference follow-up</h3><p>After an event, Ada turns messy notes, badge scans, and “remind me later” chaos into a real follow-up queue.</p></div>
              <dl>
                <div><dt>When it runs</dt><dd>After conferences, dinners, demos, and investor or partner events.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What it connects to</dt><dd>Calendar history, attendee lists, notes, photos or scans Henry provides, email threads, contact records, and priority tags.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What Henry gets back</dt><dd>A ranked follow-up list, suggested messages, owner tags, stale leads, and contacts that should be saved or merged.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Where the human stays in charge</dt><dd>Ada can organize and draft. It cannot send first-time outreach, add people to campaigns, or commit Henry to meetings without confirmation.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </article>
            <article class="usecase-card">
              <div class="uc-figure"><img src="/use-cases/action-tracker.webp" alt="Illustration: Action tracker cleanup" width="1024" height="576" loading="lazy" /></div>
              <div class="uc-topline"><span>Operations</span><h3>Action tracker cleanup</h3><p>Ada turns scattered commitments from chats, calls, and docs into owned work instead of letting them rot in the swamp.</p></div>
              <dl>
                <div><dt>When it runs</dt><dd>On demand, after meetings, or during weekly reviews.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What it connects to</dt><dd>Slack or Discord threads, meeting notes, task boards, docs, calendar context, and prior action-item history.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What Henry gets back</dt><dd>Deduplicated actions with owners, deadlines, source links, unclear items, and a clean handoff for the person responsible.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Where the human stays in charge</dt><dd>Creating internal draft tasks and summaries is fine. Reassigning people, changing official plans, or messaging stakeholders requires review unless Henry already approved that workflow.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </article>
            <article class="usecase-card">
              <div class="uc-figure"><img src="/use-cases/context-recovery.webp" alt="Illustration: Cross-session context recovery" width="1024" height="576" loading="lazy" /></div>
              <div class="uc-topline"><span>Memory</span><h3>Cross-session context recovery</h3><p>When a conversation gets long or an agent restarts, Ada rebuilds the thread from receipts instead of asking Henry to repeat himself like a haunted printer.</p></div>
              <dl>
                <div><dt>When it runs</dt><dd>After compaction, restarts, handoffs, or “what happened with this?” questions.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What it connects to</dt><dd>LCM summaries, raw message excerpts, project plans, task receipts, git history, deployment logs, and memory files.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What Henry gets back</dt><dd>A compact reconstruction of decisions, current state, evidence, blockers, and the next safe action.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Where the human stays in charge</dt><dd>Reading and summarizing are safe. Editing durable memory, changing tasks, or acting on recovered context follows the same approval boundary as the original work.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </article>
            <article class="usecase-card">
              <div class="uc-figure"><img src="/use-cases/claim-verification.webp" alt="Illustration: Public claim verification" width="1024" height="576" loading="lazy" /></div>
              <div class="uc-topline"><span>Research</span><h3>Public claim verification</h3><p>Spock checks whether a claim is true enough to use before it becomes content, strategy, or expensive confidence.</p></div>
              <dl>
                <div><dt>When it runs</dt><dd>On demand for articles, decks, market maps, investor notes, and social posts.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What it connects to</dt><dd>Primary sources, company docs, public filings, credible media, archived pages, prior research notes, and source-quality rules.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What Henry gets back</dt><dd>A source-backed answer, confidence level, contradictions, direct citations, and wording that does not overclaim.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Where the human stays in charge</dt><dd>Research can run alone. Publishing, legal-sensitive statements, customer references, or strong claims from weak evidence wait for Henry.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </article>
            <article class="usecase-card">
              <div class="uc-figure"><img src="/use-cases/cost-watch.webp" alt="Illustration: Gateway cost watch" width="1024" height="576" loading="lazy" /></div>
              <div class="uc-topline"><span>Finance</span><h3>Gateway cost watch</h3><p>Ada and Midas look for model spend, token waste, quota risk, and runaway agent loops before the bill gets funny in a bad way.</p></div>
              <dl>
                <div><dt>When it runs</dt><dd>Recurring checks and on-demand audits.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What it connects to</dt><dd>Provider usage APIs, gateway logs, cron inventory, model routing config, token ledgers, and agent-run receipts.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What Henry gets back</dt><dd>Burn-rate summaries, waste suspects, quota warnings, route recommendations, and exact evidence for anything that needs fixing.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Where the human stays in charge</dt><dd>Read-only audit and recommendations are safe. Changing production routing, cutting off agents, or rotating paid provider credentials needs approval unless a standing incident rule applies.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </article>
            <article class="usecase-card">
              <div class="uc-figure"><img src="/use-cases/evidence-doc.webp" alt="Illustration: Evidence-backed Google Doc or deck" width="1024" height="576" loading="lazy" /></div>
              <div class="uc-topline"><span>Docs</span><h3>Evidence-backed Google Doc or deck</h3><p>Ada turns raw notes into a document Henry can share, with claims grounded in sources instead of “trust me bro” vapor.</p></div>
              <dl>
                <div><dt>When it runs</dt><dd>When Henry asks for a brief, deck, memo, investor update, or customer-facing artifact.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What it connects to</dt><dd>Source docs, spreadsheets, meeting notes, transcripts, web research, prior drafts, brand rules, and Google Workspace publication tools.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What Henry gets back</dt><dd>A structured doc or deck draft, cited claims, unresolved questions, and a shareable link when publication is approved.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Where the human stays in charge</dt><dd>Drafting and internal docs are safe. Sending, sharing externally, or publishing sensitive business claims requires review.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </article>
            <article class="usecase-card">
              <div class="uc-figure"><img src="/use-cases/x-drafts.webp" alt="Illustration: X/Twitter draft from research" width="1024" height="576" loading="lazy" /></div>
              <div class="uc-topline"><span>Content</span><h3>X/Twitter draft from research</h3><p>Ada turns verified research into posts that sound like Henry, not like a B2B webinar had a nervous breakdown.</p></div>
              <dl>
                <div><dt>When it runs</dt><dd>On demand or as part of a news/content pipeline.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What it connects to</dt><dd>Source links, prior posts, Henry voice examples, publication queue, topical context, and claim-verification results.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What Henry gets back</dt><dd>Draft posts, thread options, source receipts, risk notes, and alternates tuned for punch or clarity.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Where the human stays in charge</dt><dd>Drafting and staging are safe. Posting, replying from Henry’s account, or making public claims needs approval.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </article>
            <article class="usecase-card">
              <div class="uc-figure"><img src="/use-cases/candidate-review.webp" alt="Illustration: Candidate review pack" width="1024" height="576" loading="lazy" /></div>
              <div class="uc-topline"><span>Hiring</span><h3>Candidate review pack</h3><p>Ada gathers the evidence around a hire or contractor decision so Henry can decide faster without pretending vibes are data.</p></div>
              <dl>
                <div><dt>When it runs</dt><dd>When a candidate, trial hire, or compensation decision needs review.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What it connects to</dt><dd>Submitted work, interview notes, trial-task receipts, GitHub or portfolio links, scorecards, compensation bands, and role requirements.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What Henry gets back</dt><dd>A decision pack with strengths, risks, missing evidence, compensation framing, and a clear recommendation.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Where the human stays in charge</dt><dd>Analysis and drafting are safe. Offers, compensation commitments, rejections, or access changes require Henry or the hiring owner.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </article>
            <article class="usecase-card">
              <div class="uc-figure"><img src="/use-cases/bug-diagnosis.webp" alt="Illustration: Bug diagnosis before fixing" width="1024" height="576" loading="lazy" /></div>
              <div class="uc-topline"><span>Product</span><h3>Bug diagnosis before fixing</h3><p>Ada investigates the actual failure path before touching code, because random patches are how software becomes archaeology.</p></div>
              <dl>
                <div><dt>When it runs</dt><dd>When Henry reports a broken feature, stale deploy, bad UI, or agent loop.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What it connects to</dt><dd>Repo history, logs, browser console, network traces, tests, deployment status, and screenshots.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What Henry gets back</dt><dd>Root cause, reproduction steps, smallest fix, validation output, and remaining risk.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Where the human stays in charge</dt><dd>Local reversible code fixes and tests can run. Production deploys, destructive migrations, or scope expansion need approval.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </article>
            <article class="usecase-card">
              <div class="uc-figure"><img src="/use-cases/cron-noise-control.webp" alt="Illustration: Cron noise control" width="1024" height="576" loading="lazy" /></div>
              <div class="uc-topline"><span>Governance</span><h3>Cron noise control</h3><p>Ada audits scheduled jobs so useful alerts survive and noisy little goblins stop yelling into Henry’s DMs.</p></div>
              <dl>
                <div><dt>When it runs</dt><dd>When a cron is added, repaired, or starts sending useless output.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What it connects to</dt><dd>Cron registry, delivery targets, recent outputs, channel rules, error logs, and job prompts.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What Henry gets back</dt><dd>Which jobs should stay, pause, merge, silence on success, or get stronger failure contracts.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Where the human stays in charge</dt><dd>Read-only audit and safe prompt fixes are fine. Removing important jobs, changing external delivery, or disabling production monitors needs confirmation.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </article>
            <article class="usecase-card">
              <div class="uc-figure"><img src="/use-cases/skill-loop.webp" alt="Illustration: Skill improvement loop" width="1024" height="576" loading="lazy" /></div>
              <div class="uc-topline"><span>Knowledge</span><h3>Skill improvement loop</h3><p>When an agent repeats a mistake, Ada patches the workflow itself instead of adding another sticky note to the bonfire.</p></div>
              <dl>
                <div><dt>When it runs</dt><dd>After repeated misses, hard recoveries, or Henry corrections that should persist.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What it connects to</dt><dd>Skill files, execution receipts, user corrections, failed tool outputs, docs, and regression checks.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What Henry gets back</dt><dd>A tighter reusable procedure, removed stale steps, verification rules, and a smaller chance the same nonsense happens next week.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Where the human stays in charge</dt><dd>Editing local skills and docs is safe in scope. Publishing shared skills, changing another profile, or exposing private process requires approval.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </article>
            <article class="usecase-card">
              <div class="uc-figure"><img src="/use-cases/claims-triage.webp" alt="Illustration: Claims or support triage" width="1024" height="576" loading="lazy" /></div>
              <div class="uc-topline"><span>Customer ops</span><h3>Claims or support triage</h3><p>Ada turns incoming operational cases into approve, investigate, or request-info lanes with evidence attached.</p></div>
              <dl>
                <div><dt>When it runs</dt><dd>When a queue of claims, support requests, or customer build tasks needs first-pass sorting.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What it connects to</dt><dd>Case records, policy or product rules, previous correspondence, attachments, customer context, and escalation criteria.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What Henry gets back</dt><dd>A triaged list, reason for each decision, missing information, draft responses, and escalation flags.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Where the human stays in charge</dt><dd>Classification and drafts can run. Customer-facing decisions, payments, production data changes, or policy exceptions require approval.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </article>
            <article class="usecase-card">
              <div class="uc-figure"><img src="/use-cases/contact-saving.webp" alt="Illustration: Contact saving and cleanup" width="1024" height="576" loading="lazy" /></div>
              <div class="uc-topline"><span>Personal ops</span><h3>Contact saving and cleanup</h3><p>When Henry says “save this contact,” Ada treats Apple/iCloud Contacts as the source of truth and avoids inventing a second address book.</p></div>
              <dl>
                <div><dt>When it runs</dt><dd>On demand when Henry sends a person, number, email, or follow-up instruction.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What it connects to</dt><dd>Apple/iCloud Contacts, provided details, conversation context, duplicate checks, and relationship notes when allowed.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What Henry gets back</dt><dd>A saved or updated contact, duplicate warning, and the exact fields changed.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Where the human stays in charge</dt><dd>Creating or updating the intended local contact is safe when requested. Messaging the person, enriching externally, or changing unrelated records needs approval.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </article>
            <article class="usecase-card">
              <div class="uc-figure"><img src="/use-cases/plan-grill.webp" alt="Illustration: Plan grill" width="1024" height="576" loading="lazy" /></div>
              <div class="uc-topline"><span>Strategy</span><h3>Plan grill</h3><p>Ada pressure-tests a plan before the team spends a week executing something that sounded smart in Slack.</p></div>
              <dl>
                <div><dt>When it runs</dt><dd>When Henry asks whether a plan, feature, business idea, or workflow makes sense.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What it connects to</dt><dd>The proposal, market or customer context, constraints, prior decisions, evidence sources, and relevant operator skills.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>What Henry gets back</dt><dd>What is strong, what is vague, what will break, what to test first, and the smallest next move.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Where the human stays in charge</dt><dd>Critique and planning are safe. Starting external work, assigning people, spending money, or making commitments requires confirmation.</dd></div>
              </dl>
            </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="catalog" class="catalog" aria-labelledby="catalog-title">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">100 public-safe workflow rows</p>
          <h2 id="catalog-title">Filter by agent, risk, proof, or lane</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="filters" aria-label="Catalog filters">
          <input id="atlas-search" type="search" placeholder="Search category, agent, risk, input, or tool path" />
          <select id="agent-filter"><option value="">All agents</option>{atlas.agents.map((agent) => <option value={agent}>{agent}</option>)}</select>
          <select id="proof-filter"><option value="">All proof states</option><option>Verified</option><option>Inferred</option></select>
        </div>
        <p id="catalog-count" class="count">100 workflows shown</p>
        <div class="workflow-grid" id="workflow-grid">
          {atlas.rows.map((row) => (
            <article class="workflow-card" data-search={`${row.id} ${row.category} ${row.agents.join(' ')} ${row.evidence} ${row.risk} ${row.inputs} ${row.toolPath}`.toLowerCase()} data-agents={row.agents.join('|')} data-proof={row.evidence}>
              <div class="card-top"><span>{row.id}</span><strong>{row.evidence}</strong></div>
              <h3>{row.category}</h3>
              <p>{row.toolPath}</p>
              <dl>
                <div><dt>Agents</dt><dd>{row.agents.join(', ')}</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Inputs</dt><dd>{row.inputs || 'Declared sources'}</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Risk</dt><dd>{row.risk}</dd></div>
              </dl>
              <small>{row.approval}</small>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="video" aria-labelledby="video-title">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">Script-to-video runway</p>
          <h2 id="video-title">Three production treatments ready for rendering</h2>
          <p>The v1 release includes storyboards and motion contracts. Final generated video is a separate production pass so exact labels and continuity can be verified frame by frame.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="video-grid">
          {atlas.videoTreatments.map((treatment) => <article><span>{treatment.duration}</span><h3>{treatment.title}</h3><p>{treatment.cta}</p></article>)}
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>
    <Footer />
    <script>
      const search = document.getElementById('atlas-search') as HTMLInputElement | null;
      const agent = document.getElementById('agent-filter') as HTMLSelectElement | null;
      const proof = document.getElementById('proof-filter') as HTMLSelectElement | null;
      const cards = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.workflow-card'));
      const count = document.getElementById('catalog-count');
      function applyFilters() {
        const q = (search?.value || '').trim().toLowerCase();
        const selectedAgent = agent?.value || '';
        const selectedProof = proof?.value || '';
        let visible = 0;
        cards.forEach((card) => {
          const haystack = card.dataset.search || '';
          const agents = card.dataset.agents || '';
          const proofState = card.dataset.proof || '';
          const show = (!q || haystack.includes(q)) && (!selectedAgent || agents.split('|').includes(selectedAgent)) && (!selectedProof || proofState === selectedProof);
          card.hidden = !show;
          if (show) visible += 1;
        });
        if (count) count.textContent = `${visible} workflows shown`;
      }
      [search, agent, proof].forEach((el) => el?.addEventListener('input', applyFilters));
    </script>
    <style>
      .workflow-atlas { width:min(1180px, calc(100% - 32px)); margin:0 auto; padding:56px 0 80px; }
      .atlas-hero { border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:36px; padding:clamp(28px, 6vw, 72px); background:radial-gradient(circle at top left, color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 22%, transparent), transparent 36%), linear-gradient(135deg, color-mix(in srgb, var(--panel) 92%, transparent), color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg) 95%, transparent)); box-shadow:0 30px 90px color-mix(in srgb, var(--glow) 18%, transparent); }
      .eyebrow { color:var(--accent); text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:.18em; font-size:12px; font-weight:900; margin:0 0 14px; }
      h1 { max-width:980px; font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif; font-size:clamp(46px, 8vw, 96px); line-height:.93; letter-spacing:-.055em; margin:0; }
      .lede { max-width:820px; color:var(--muted); font-size:clamp(18px, 2.2vw, 23px); line-height:1.55; margin:28px 0 0; }
      .hero-actions { display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:12px; margin-top:30px; }
      .hero-actions a { display:inline-flex; align-items:center; min-height:48px; padding:0 18px; border-radius:999px; background:var(--accent); color:#120b04; font-weight:900; text-decoration:none; text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:.08em; font-size:12px; }
      .hero-actions a.secondary { background:transparent; color:var(--text); border:1px solid var(--border); }
      .stat-grid, .category-grid, .recipe-grid, .video-grid { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(190px,1fr)); gap:14px; margin-top:18px; }
      .stat-grid article, .category-grid article, .recipe-grid article, .video-grid article, .workflow-card, .notice { border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:24px; background:color-mix(in srgb, var(--panel) 84%, transparent); padding:20px; }
      .stat-grid article span, .category-grid article span, .video-grid article span { color:var(--accent); font-size:12px; font-weight:900; letter-spacing:.14em; text-transform:uppercase; }
      .stat-grid article strong { display:block; font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif; font-size:44px; margin-top:8px; }
      .notice { margin:18px 0 52px; color:var(--muted); line-height:1.7; }
      .notice strong { color:var(--text); }
      section { margin-top:64px; }
      .section-head { max-width:780px; margin-bottom:22px; }
      .section-head h2 { font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif; font-size:clamp(34px,5vw,58px); line-height:1; letter-spacing:-.04em; margin:0; }
      .section-head p:not(.eyebrow) { color:var(--muted); line-height:1.65; }
      .visual-grid { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(280px,1fr)); gap:18px; }
      figure { margin:0; border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:24px; padding:12px; background:color-mix(in srgb, var(--panel) 70%, transparent); }
      figure img { display:block; width:100%; height:auto; border-radius:16px; background:#071018; }
      figcaption { margin:10px 4px 2px; color:var(--muted); font-weight:800; }
      .category-grid h3, .recipe-grid h3, .video-grid h3, .workflow-card h3 { margin:10px 0; font-size:20px; }
      .category-grid p, .recipe-grid p, .video-grid p, .workflow-card p, .workflow-card small, .category-grid small, .recipe-grid small { color:var(--muted); line-height:1.55; }
      .filters { display:grid; grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr) 170px 170px; gap:12px; margin:18px 0 10px; }
      .filters input, .filters select { min-height:48px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:14px; background:color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg) 78%, transparent); color:var(--text); padding:0 14px; }
      .count { color:var(--muted); font-weight:800; }
      .workflow-grid { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(290px,1fr)); gap:14px; }
      .card-top { display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:space-between; gap:12px; }
      .card-top span, .card-top strong { color:var(--accent); font-size:12px; font-weight:900; letter-spacing:.12em; text-transform:uppercase; }
      .workflow-card dl { display:grid; gap:8px; margin:16px 0; }
      .workflow-card div { min-width:0; }
      .workflow-card dt { color:var(--accent); font-size:11px; font-weight:900; text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:.12em; }
      .workflow-card dd { margin:2px 0 0; color:var(--text); overflow-wrap:anywhere; }
      .usecase-grid { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(340px,1fr)); gap:16px; }
      .usecase-card { border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:24px; background:color-mix(in srgb, var(--panel) 84%, transparent); padding:18px; }
      .uc-figure img { display:block; width:100%; height:auto; border-radius:16px; background:#071018; }
      .uc-topline { margin-top:14px; }
      .uc-topline span { color:var(--accent); font-size:12px; font-weight:900; letter-spacing:.14em; text-transform:uppercase; }
      .uc-topline h3 { margin:8px 0; font-size:20px; }
      .uc-topline p { color:var(--muted); line-height:1.55; margin:0; }
      .usecase-card dl { display:grid; gap:10px; margin:14px 0 0; }
      .usecase-card dt { color:var(--accent); font-size:11px; font-weight:900; text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:.12em; }
      .usecase-card dd { margin:2px 0 0; color:var(--text); line-height:1.5; overflow-wrap:anywhere; }
      @media (max-width:720px) { .workflow-atlas { width:min(100% - 24px, 1180px); padding-top:28px; } .filters { grid-template-columns:1fr; } h1 { font-size:48px; } .atlas-hero { border-radius:26px; } }
    </style>
  </body>
</html>
